# idle-screens-wayland — swayidle integration (sway / labwc / wayfire / Raspberry Pi OS)
#
# Unlike hypridle, swayidle has NO config file: it is configured entirely by its
# command line, wherever your session launches it. So there is nothing to patch —
# you edit the launch site. Find yours with:
#
#   ps -o args= -C swayidle
#   grep -rl swayidle ~/.config/labwc /etc/xdg/labwc ~/.config/autostart \
#       /etc/xdg/autostart ~/.config/systemd/user 2>/dev/null
#
# Common launch sites:
#   labwc      ~/.config/labwc/autostart   (copy /etc/xdg/labwc/autostart first;
#                                           never edit /etc — an update reverts it)
#   sway       ~/.config/sway/config       (exec swayidle ...)
#   wayfire    ~/.config/wayfire.ini       [autostart]
#   systemd    ~/.config/systemd/user/swayidle.service
#
# Or run: ./packaging/swayidle/install-swayidle.sh
#
# ORDERING MATTERS. There is only ONE swayidle process and it owns every timeout,
# so extend the existing line — do not start a second instance. The saver timeout
# must be strictly LESS than the display-blank (wlopm/DPMS) timeout, or the panel
# powers off seconds after the overlay appears and the saver runs behind a dark
# screen. Raspberry Pi OS's stock blank timeout is short enough to hit this.
# Recommended: saver 150 s, blank 900 s.
#
# NB the kill pattern: the kernel truncates a process name to 15 chars
# ("idle-screens-wa"), so `pkill -x idle-screens-wayland` never matches and the
# saver is never dismissed. Match the command line; the [i] bracket stops pkill's
# own shell from matching (and killing) itself.

swayidle -w \
  timeout 150 'idle-screens-wayland' \
    resume 'pkill -TERM -f "[i]dle-screens-wayland"' \
  timeout 900 'wlopm --off \*' \
    resume 'wlopm --on \*' &

# Kiosk (mouse does not dismiss, display never blanks): drop BOTH the
# `resume 'pkill ...'` and the wlopm pair. Leaving wlopm in place would sleep the
# panel behind a saver that nothing will dismiss.
#
# swayidle -w \
#   timeout 150 'pgrep -f "[i]dle-screens-wayland" || idle-screens-wayland --kiosk' &
#
# Exit manually: pkill -TERM -f '[i]dle-screens-wayland'

# After editing, restart swayidle — `labwc --reconfigure` does NOT re-run
# autostart, so a reconfigure is not enough:
#   pkill -x swayidle && setsid sh -c '<your new swayidle line>' &
# or simply log out and back in.
